    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="timeline">A realistic timeline</h2>
  <p>
    There is no single honest week or month count that fits everyone, and any guide that hands you an exact
    number is guessing. What holds up across most people who actually break in: motion graphics and 2D character
    animation tend to reach a hireable first reel faster than VFX or full 3D pipeline work, because the software
    learning curve and the expected production discipline are both lower to start.
  </p>
  <p>
    Someone with a design or fine-arts background, or existing After Effects familiarity, often reaches an
    interview-ready motion graphics or 2D reel in a focused stretch of a few months. Someone building 3D or VFX
    skills from a completely unrelated background more realistically needs closer to a year of consistent,
    structured practice, whether through an institute program or disciplined self-study, before a reel is strong
    enough to clear studio review. Judge your own pace against the track you picked, not against a course
    provider's promised timeline.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>
  <p>
    Do not enroll in another software course before you have picked one track and sketched what your first
    three demo-reel pieces will actually be. That single decision moves your odds further than another month of
    scattered tutorials across every specialization at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    Moving toward earlier financial freedom through this path comes down to the same thing it always does: the
    right specialization for your actual strengths, visible proof through a genuinely focused reel, and clear
    understanding of where the current jobs, service work, original content, or gaming, are really concentrated.
    If you want a second opinion on whether animation genuinely fits your interests and constraints, over a
    closer field like video editing, graphic design, or game development,
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career
    guidance</a> can help you map the entry path that fits your situation, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a> if you are
    still deciding whether this is genuinely your lane. You can also explore the wider
    <a href="/career-skills-compass">Career and Skills Compass</a> or browse more
    <a href="/career-resources?topic=skills&type=roadmap">skill roadmaps</a> if a different creative or technical
    direction fits better.
  </p>

